Regression test for the sockbplog reply-buffer use-after-free.

A replicant ships its bplog to the master over a "sockbplog" appsock and then
waits for the commit rc on that same socket.  On the master, the block
processor writes that rc back over the appsock's own buffer (sess->target->sb),
from a different thread than the appsock handler.

handle_sockbplog_request_session() used to return as soon as the session was
dispatched, handing the session to the writer thread.  The appsock framework
then tore the connection buffer down, so any reply landing after that return
wrote into freed memory.

The test forces the writer to reply long after the appsock is done, using
tunables that already exist:

  sleep_before_dispatch (master)       stalls the block processor before it
                                       runs the transaction, so the reply is
                                       written STALL seconds late.

  SOSQL_MAX_COMMIT_WAIT_SEC (lrl)      how long the replicant waits for that
                                       rc.  Set well below STALL so the
                                       replicant has certainly given up, and
                                       closed its end of the sockbplog socket,
                                       before the reply is written.

  DISABLE_SERVER_SOCKPOOL (lrl)        so the replicant's disconnect really
                                       closes the socket instead of donating it
                                       back to the pool.  We want the master's
                                       appsock to see EOF and free its buffer,
                                       which is the condition under test.

Without the fix the master writes the reply into a buffer the appsock has
already freed.  That is heap corruption, so it can surface as a core (which the
harness picks up), as a hang, or as damage that only shows up on later traffic
-- which is why the test also replays a clean, verifiable workload afterwards
and checks the cluster is still healthy.

With the fix the appsock blocks in osql_sess_socket_wait_io() until
osql_sess_close() signals that the writer is done with the buffer.  That wait is
bounded at 60s and logs "timed out waiting for writer to release socket" if it
expires; the test fails if that message appears, since it means the session
degraded back to the racy behaviour.
